From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Barnes <hello@jonbarnes.dev>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 17:20:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Every candidate rule for a solid draw, on the Experiment row

Patch 0011 of the renderer series (OoT: Recompiled).

Patch 0010 said a cut-out is not a translucent surface and picked one way to recognize one: the
coverage cut or the alpha compare. On the user's trees that changed nothing in either position,
which is itself a result: those draws do not declare themselves that way.

Rather than guess a second discriminator and ship another build to find out, every candidate the
microcode actually offers goes on the Experiment row and the picture answers. The user asked for
exactly this: "possible to add every possibily of fixes as settings to cycle through so that i
can cycle through and see which one yields best results or fixes the issue?"

The last option is not a candidate fix. It treats every translucent draw as solid, which is
wrong for water and for every glow in the game, and it is there as a control: if the trees take
light under it and under nothing else, the mechanism is confirmed and only the discriminator is
wrong; if they do not take light even under it, the cause is not the classification at all and
the search moves to the raster order and the depth mask.

With the ray tracing row Off none of this runs: the classification sits inside the traced
projection's branch.
---
diff --git a/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp b/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp
--- a/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp
+++ b/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp
@@ -1811,11 +1811,45 @@ namespace RT64 {
                         // back. The Experiment row's "Cut-outs translucent" puts 0006's rule
                         // back for a side by side, which is how the last few lighting questions
                         // were settled.
+                        // WHICH RULE DECIDES THAT AN XLU DRAW IS REALLY SOLID GEOMETRY (patch
+                        // 0011, the user's ask of 2026-09-30: "possible to add every possibily of
+                        // fixes as settings to cycle through so that i can cycle through and see
+                        // which one yields best results ... brainstorm every possible edge case").
+                        //
+                        // Patch 0010 picked one discriminator, the coverage cut or the alpha
+                        // compare, and on his trees it changed nothing either way, which means
+                        // those draws do not declare themselves that way. Rather than guess a
+                        // second time, every candidate the microcode actually offers is on the
+                        // Experiment row and he cycles until the picture answers. Each is a real
+                        // statement a draw makes about itself:
+                        //
+                        //   default  the coverage cut or the alpha compare: whole texel or none
+                        //   8        nothing is solid, which is patch 0006 exactly as it was
+                        //   9        it WRITES depth, so it occludes, so it is not a glow
+                        //   10       it READS depth, the weaker half of the same idea
+                        //   11       coverage stands in for alpha, the other whole-or-none form
+                        //   12       the blend is not FORCED, so it is not really a blend
+                        //   13       everything, which is not a candidate fix but the CONTROL:
+                        //            if the trees light under this and under nothing else the
+                        //            mechanism is confirmed and only the discriminator is wrong,
+                        //            and if they do not light even under this then the cause is
+                        //            not the classification at all and the search moves to the
+                        //            raster order and the depth mask. It will be wrong for water
+                        //            and for every glow, which is the point: it is a probe.
                         const uint32_t acMode = otherMode.alphaCompare();
-                        const bool cutoutDraw = otherMode.cvgXAlpha() || (acMode == G_AC_THRESHOLD) || (acMode == G_AC_DITHER);
-                        const bool cutoutTranslucent = (float(rtResources->rtParams.tuneExperiment.x) == 8.0f);
+                        const int cutoutRule = int(rtResources->rtParams.tuneExperiment.x);
+                        bool solidDraw;
+                        switch (cutoutRule) {
+                            case 8:  solidDraw = false; break;
+                            case 9:  solidDraw = otherMode.zUpd(); break;
+                            case 10: solidDraw = otherMode.zCmp(); break;
+                            case 11: solidDraw = otherMode.alphaCvgSel(); break;
+                            case 12: solidDraw = !otherMode.forceBlend(); break;
+                            case 13: solidDraw = true; break;
+                            default: solidDraw = otherMode.cvgXAlpha() || (acMode == G_AC_THRESHOLD) || (acMode == G_AC_DITHER); break;
+                        }
                         // A translucent draw (the XLU z mode) gets a mask no ray carries (patch 0006).
-                        if ((otherMode.zMode() == ZMODE_XLU) && !(cutoutDraw && !cutoutTranslucent)) {
+                        if ((otherMode.zMode() == ZMODE_XLU) && !solidDraw) {
                             raytracing.queryMask = TranslucentRayQueryMask;
                         }
                         else {
